NOTE: This is still all a proposal. It's not been written yet, and if
it is in progress in trunk/ then you are warned against using it until
this warning goes away.

=====================================
xproc - The Libxc concurrency library
=====================================

Concurrency is difficult. Very difficult. Implementing threads, on the other
hand, is dead simple if your platform supports hardware signals. The decision
for Libxc to exclude thread functionality was not an easy one.

On the one hand threads are available on almost all platforms and so
wrapping up the different implementations with a single unified interface
is trivial. On the other hand threads are certain to almost always cause
difficult-to-track bugs that show up only months or years after release.
Putting a thin wrapper around a foundational threads interface such as
pthreads doesn't solve any of the problems of multi-threaded programs
although the added layer, even if trivially simple, may introduce bugs
via subtle threaded behaviour in the face of race conditions or similar.

The C1x standard doesn't help matters either; the addition of thread
primitives (atomic types, etc) does little to alleviate the problems
associated with a shared-memory concurrency model. Some languages introduce
concurrency as a primitive via a immutable memory model (such as Erlang).
Unfortunately the libxc team (myself) doesn't have the opportunity of
utilising a different language; the whole premise of libxc is a cross-
platform C library. We won't very well be advancing the cause of making
C more powerful by advocating a different language.

Even if we had the luxury of introducing an immutable shared-memory model,
the audience that libxc is aimed at, the average C developer, might find
the idea of immutable memory a strange and alien concept. Even if they
didn't, immutable memory (whether shared or not) requires the language to
support garbage collection: when A and B are immutable and you need to
append B to A then a new variable C will be created whether the programmer
requires it or not as A cannot be modified. Thus, A will have to be 
destroyed implicitly at some point.

Using threads opens up more than the shared-memory problem. Determining
deadlocks before they occur is NP-hard; it is not a solvable problem no
matter how much computational power is available. Deadlocks are a shared-
resource problem (a superset of the shared-memory problem) that can be
easily avoided by enforcing ordered deadlock acquisition; however that
approach means much diligence by the programmer. In any event, a project
that has churn over the years (programmers come and programmers go) will
sooner or later be modified by a programmer with less than full diligence
at which point the entire exercise was futile.

Other than deadlocks, races, access violations and non-deterministic
interleaving resulting in unpredictable results, what good are threads?
A networked program that talks to many hosts at once can, for example,
using select() on the socket descriptors returned by xsock_connect()
and xsock_listen() to receive and/or transmit data simultaneously to
all of them (assuming the processing power is available). Threads aren't
needed for that.

Well, threads bring one valuable item to the programmers toolkit - they
bring concurrency. It's true that concurrency can be found in other 
languages in a much neater and more understandable form, it is the C
programmer's toolkit that we are primarily interested in enhancing.

Which brings us to libxc and its thread support: libxc does not support
threads. What libxc does support is concurrency, but not by using threads.
It is true that the underlying primitive might use (for example) pthreads
to produce the concurrency, it would be wrong to call the concurrency
primitives in libxc 'threads'. Libxc supports the concept of 'processes',
which are somewhat like threads in that they execute independently of the
calling program, but that's where the similarities end.

For one thing, while threads have full access to the caller's memory,
processes have none - they run in their own memory space and merely 
inherit the callers environment. Any changes made to this environment
remain visible to only the child process. The unix gurus reading this
are probably going "Ah well, they reinvented fork(), well done!" but the
Microsoft Windows developers are probably wondering how the hell does
libxc implement fork() on the Win32 API.

Good question. Unix fork() is a quick painless procedure that produces a
copy-on-write child process from the parent process. Microsoft Windows
has CreateProcess() that requires a new program as the argument. Cygwin
has made some headway into a fork() clone that uses a non-copy-on-write
process which is as slow as it sounds (slower, actually).

The Libxc proposal for sub-processes is actually a kludge (but a fast 
one) on Win32. The unix version of libxc uses fork() to produce the
child process in a fast and painless manner. The Win32 version of libxc
executes the current process with a specially formatted command-line string
that is recognised by the libxc argument processing functions. When it is
determined that the current copy was invoked as a process the libxc xproc
functions perform a non-return jump to the specified function.

It looks something like this:
////////////////////////////////////////
// The entry point into the child process
int proc_entry_point (char **args)
{
   // Turn all the string arguments in argv[0]..argv[n] into values
   ...
   // Perform all processing required.
   ...
   // Send a message to the parent process
   char txmsg[] = "Message from child to parent";
   size_t bytes_written = xproc_send (0, txmsg, sizeof tsmsg);
   ...
   // Read the message queue for a message from any process
   char input[] = "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx";
   size_t bytes_read = xproc_recv (input, sizeof input);
   ...
   // This process ends when this function ends
   return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}

// Create the child process
size_t handle = xproc_start_func (func_name, "arg1", "arg2", NULL);
// OR use a different program to create the child process
size_t handle = xproc_start_proc (proc_name, func_name, "arg1", "arg2", NULL);

// Send data to the child process
char txmsg[] = "Message from Parent to Child";
size_t xproc_send (handle, txmsg, sizeof txmsg);

// Read the message queue for a message from any process
char input[] = "xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx";
size_t bytes_read = xproc_recv (input, sizeof input);

////////////////////////////////////////

It is suggested that the child/parent communications also include 
a watchdog/heartbeat message so that the child can decide to end if
the parent ends for any reason. Any other approach to ending orphan
processes involves maintaining a list independent of child and parent
that is maintained by a third process; this is a method which, due to
the addition of a third party, introduces unnecessary complexity.

The processes, without programmer action, run a single additional thread
that listens for messages and pops them into a queue. The messages are
received via TCP using xsock. Upon starting and before the entry point
is reached, the child process sets up the thread, retrieves the parents
ip:port, decides on a port to use and sends the parent the ip:port that
it will receive messages on. The parent only returns from xproc_start()
once it has the communication details for the child. Additionally, the
listening thread on the child can watch for an end_process message and 
forcibly close the child process.

Each message is encoded in a stream with [stx][8-byte-length][payload],
so each message sent by one party is received either whole or not at
all by the other party. That is, messages are atomic in transmission;
the programmer need not try to reassemble full messages from pieces that
were received. No retransmits nor acknowledgments are performed.

Of course, this is all local to a single machine. A proposed loosely 
coupled distributed system built on this functionality is one in which
the instruction to spawn a process is actually transferred to a locally
running server which decides whether to spawn the process locally or
to transfer the request to one of a list of different remote machines.

This allows the spawn invocation to cause a process to be started on
a remote machine without the caller needing to know or care about where
the actual process is.




















































